{"date": "2026-10-26", "post_id": 1500112, "title": "M42: The Great Nebula in Orion", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_Nebula\">Great Nebula in Orion</a>, an immense, nearby <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/exploring-the-birth-of-stars/\">starbirth region</a>, is one the most famous of all <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">astronomical nebulas</a>. Here, glowing gas surrounds hot young stars at the edge of an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-october-23-orion-head-to-toe/\">immense</a> interstellar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-january-29-barnard-68-dark-molecular-cloud/\">molecular cloud</a> only 1500 <a href=\"http://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/cosmic_distance.html\">light-years</a> away. In the featured deep image in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LRGB\">assigned colors</a> highlighted by emission in <a href=\"https://www.lanl.gov/science-engineering/periodic-table/element/hydrogen\">hydrogen</a>, wisps and sheets of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1999-may-09-fractal-interstellar-dust-up-close/\">dust</a> and gas are particularly evident. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-november-4-the-great-orion-nebula-m42/\">Great Nebula in Orion</a> can be found with the <a href=\"https://c.tenor.com/JhvJXq30yA4AAAAC/tenor.gif\">unaided eye</a> near the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2008-october-15-camera-orion/\">easily identifiable</a> belt of three stars in the popular <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/constellations/\">constellation</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_%28constellation%29\">Orion</a>. In addition to housing a bright <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_cluster\">open cluster</a> of stars known as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-august-5-trapezium-at-the-heart-of-orion/\">Trapezium</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-february-13-infrared-orion-from-wise/\">Orion Nebula</a> contains many <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/\">stellar nurseries</a>. These nurseries contain much <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMB2VR0087w\">hydrogen</a> gas, hot young stars, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1996-october-17-proplyds-infant-solar-systems/\">proplyds</a>, and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-march-24-dust-pillar-of-the-carina-nebula/\">stellar jets</a> spewing material at high speeds. Also known as <a href=\"https://youtu.be/2w8wwUqC4Xc\">M42</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1998-april-21-water-from-orion/\">Orion Nebula</a> spans about 40 <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/\">light years</a> and is located in the same <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2008-june-6-two-armed-spiral-milky-way/\">spiral arm</a> of <a href=\"http://www.atlasoftheuniverse.com/galaxy.html\">our Galaxy</a> as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space", "credit": "<a href=\"mailto:fumettopolirc at gmail dot com\">Roberto Palaia</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"mailto:fumettopolirc at gmail dot com\">Roberto Palaia</a>", "alt": "A starfield features a large and colorful nebula in its center, filled with dark dust and glowing gas in red and blue.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg?w=2000&h=2090&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 26 - M42: The Great Nebula in Orion</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: The Great Nebula in Orion, an immense, nearby starbirth region, is one the most famous of all astronomical nebulas. Here, glowing gas surrounds hot young stars at the edge of an immense interstellar molecular cloud only 1500 light-years away. In the featured deep image in assigned colors highlighted by emission in hydrogen, wisps and sheets of dust and gas are particularly evident. The Great Nebula in Orion can be found with the unaided eye near the easily identifiable belt of three stars in the popular constellation Orion. In addition to housing a bright open cluster of stars known as the Trapezium, the Orion Nebula contains many stellar nurseries. These nurseries contain much hydrogen gas, hot young stars, proplyds, and stellar jets spewing material at high speeds. Also known as M42, the Orion Nebula spans about 40 light years and is located in the same spiral arm of our Galaxy as the Sun.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"m42: the great nebula in orion\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"M42: The Great Nebula in Orion\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg?w=2000&#038;h=2090&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg?w=2000&#038;h=2090&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A starfield features a large and colorful nebula in its center, filled with dark dust and glowing gas in red and blue.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-26T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-26T08:24:17-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"M42: The Great Nebula in Orion\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg?w=2000&#038;h=2090&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 26<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/OrionSword_Palaia_2000.jpg\" alt=\"A starfield features a large and colorful nebula in its center, filled with dark dust and glowing gas in red and blue.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> M42: The Great Nebula in Orion </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:fumettopolirc at gmail dot com\">Roberto Palaia</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_Nebula\">Great Nebula in Orion</a>, an immense, nearby <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/exploring-the-birth-of-stars/\">starbirth region</a>, is one the most famous of all <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">astronomical nebulas</a>. Here, glowing gas surrounds hot young stars at the edge of an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-october-23-orion-head-to-toe/\">immense</a> interstellar <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-january-29-barnard-68-dark-molecular-cloud/\">molecular cloud</a> only 1500 <a href=\"http://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/cosmic_distance.html\">light-years</a> away. In the featured deep image in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LRGB\">assigned colors</a> highlighted by emission in <a href=\"https://www.lanl.gov/science-engineering/periodic-table/element/hydrogen\">hydrogen</a>, wisps and sheets of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1999-may-09-fractal-interstellar-dust-up-close/\">dust</a> and gas are particularly evident. The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-november-4-the-great-orion-nebula-m42/\">Great Nebula in Orion</a> can be found with the <a href=\"https://c.tenor.com/JhvJXq30yA4AAAAC/tenor.gif\">unaided eye</a> near the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2008-october-15-camera-orion/\">easily identifiable</a> belt of three stars in the popular <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/constellations/\">constellation</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_%28constellation%29\">Orion</a>. In addition to housing a bright <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_cluster\">open cluster</a> of stars known as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-august-5-trapezium-at-the-heart-of-orion/\">Trapezium</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-february-13-infrared-orion-from-wise/\">Orion Nebula</a> contains many <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/\">stellar nurseries</a>. These nurseries contain much <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMB2VR0087w\">hydrogen</a> gas, hot young stars, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1996-october-17-proplyds-infant-solar-systems/\">proplyds</a>, and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-march-24-dust-pillar-of-the-carina-nebula/\">stellar jets</a> spewing material at high speeds. Also known as <a href=\"https://youtu.be/2w8wwUqC4Xc\">M42</a>, the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1998-april-21-water-from-orion/\">Orion Nebula</a> spans about 40 <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/\">light years</a> and is located in the same <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2008-june-6-two-armed-spiral-milky-way/\">spiral arm</a> of <a href=\"http://www.atlasoftheuniverse.com/galaxy.html\">our Galaxy</a> as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261026/html"}